e fans might spin and the card gets warm—sustained 4K 60fps HEVC decoding + HDR processing can pull 20-40W—but that’s a **tiny fraction** of the 200-300W+ the card pulls during actual 3D rendering. The GPU’s main rendering engine is basically idle, sipping power just to keep the display interface alive. ### Think of it this way: - **Making the movie:** The render farm (full of GPUs) struggled for months, doing exactly the kind of real-time-style 3D rendering you’re thinking of, just absurdly slowly per frame. That’s where the “real rendering” lives. - **Watching the movie:** Your GPU plays the video file of that finished work. It’s like the difference between a chef (the render farm) cooking a meal from scratch over hours, and you (your home GPU) just heating up the finished dish in a microwave. The microwave gets warm, the fan runs, but you’re not doing the chef’s work. **In a game:** your GPU is both the chef and the microwave, making the meal from scratch in the 3 milliseconds before you’re ready to eat each bite. That’s the struggle. So yes, graphics cards are used to render animated movies—but those cards live in a data center, running for days, to produce a video file. The card in your PC just plays back that pre-rendered footage. The fact that both are called “graphics cards” doesn’t mean they’re doing the same job when you’re watching the movie. The heavy lifting was already done. That’s why it’s so “easy” to play, even if the fan tells you it’s not totally effortless.
